---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageTitle from '../components/PageTitle.astro';
import Apparatus from '../components/Apparatus.astro';
---
<Base title="Method" current="method"
  description="How these projects handle uncertainty: the shared visual grammar for evidence and absence, the reproducibility discipline behind every dataset, and the romanisation policy used across the site.">
  <PageTitle title="Method"
    lede="Three independent projects, one commitment. Colonial-era and premodern sources are fragmentary, contested and often hostile to the people they describe, and most computational work smooths that away. These projects represent it instead. This page states the grammar once, so that every figure on the site can be read without an explanation of its own." />

  <section class="band band--flush">
    <div class="wrap">

      <section class="section">
        <h2><span class="mark">Uncertainty</span>A mark says how good the evidence is, not how important the thing is</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Every dated figure on this site uses the same four marks. They encode the evidential status of a
            record, and they are drawn rather than footnoted, so a reader can see the quality of the evidence
            at the same moment as the pattern it makes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="grammar">
          <div class="g-row">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 120 22" role="img" aria-label="A solid bar."><rect x="2" y="5" width="116" height="12" fill="var(--b2)"></rect></svg>
            <div><b>Solid</b><span>An explicitly dated record. The dates are attested in a source, not inferred.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="g-row">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 120 22" role="img" aria-label="A dashed outlined bar."><rect x="2" y="5" width="116" height="12" fill="none" stroke="var(--b2)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="5 4"></rect></svg>
            <div><b>Dashed</b><span>A window derived from a floruit, an approximate date, or a reign. The mark shows a range of possibility, not a life.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="g-row">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 120 22" role="img" aria-label="A hatched bar.">
              <defs><pattern id="hatchlegend" width="6" height="6" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse" patternTransform="rotate(45)"><rect width="3" height="6" fill="var(--b5)"></rect></pattern></defs>
              <rect x="2" y="5" width="116" height="12" fill="url(#hatchlegend)"></rect>
            </svg>
            <div><b>Hatched</b><span>The record rests on a single source. It is in the dataset, and it is not corroborated.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="g-row">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 120 22" role="img" aria-label="A dotted horizontal line.">
              <line x1="2" y1="11" x2="118" y2="11" stroke="var(--void)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="2 5"></line>
            </svg>
            <div><b>Dotted, annotated</b><span>An absence shown in place. Where the evidence bar admits nothing, the figure says so on the axis rather than closing the gap.</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <Apparatus label="Why the grammar carries no colour">
          <p>
            Pattern, not hue, carries the evidential meaning, so every figure remains legible in greyscale, in
            print, and to readers with colour-vision deficiency. Colour is used only to separate categories
            that have no evidential ordering, such as the six cohorts in Buddhist Bridges, and that palette was
            machine-checked for colour-vision safety before use.
          </p>
        </Apparatus>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2><span class="mark">Dates</span>Precision is recorded, never invented</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            A date that a source does not give is not supplied. Records carry a precision field, and the
            notation is kept intact everywhere the record appears, including in tables and on person pages.
          </p>
          <ul class="notation">
            <li><code>704–787</code> — attested birth and death.</li>
            <li><code>fl. 671–695 ?</code> — a floruit window; the question mark is part of the record.</li>
            <li><code>b. 1933</code> — one attested date only.</li>
            <li><code>undated</code> — no safe date. Such records are listed in the register and are never placed on an axis.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2><span class="mark">Absence</span>A zero under stated criteria is a finding; an unsearched gap is not</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Two different kinds of nothing appear in this work, and they are labelled differently. A zero
            reached by applying stated inclusion criteria to sources that were actually searched is a result:
            the five centuries of Chosŏn in which no India–Korea Buddhist mediator meets the evidence bar, and
            the decade of Indian newspaper coverage in which the words Hallyu or Korean wave appear once in
            10,146 Korea-related articles. A gap produced by material that was never coded, never digitised, or
            never licensed is a limit of the work, and is named as such: Issue 2 of 長恨 has not been coded for
            place, and no licensed full text covers the Indian press before 2005.
          </p>
          <p>
            Candidates examined and rejected stay in the dataset as documented negatives rather than being
            deleted, so that the boundary of a claim can be inspected. Legendary material is labelled and
            excluded from analysis by default rather than quietly dropped.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Apparatus label="On what a dataset does not contain">
          <p>
            Following the practice of the Prosopography of the Byzantine World: it should never be assumed
            that what a dataset does not contain does not exist. Every absence on this site is either a
            documented negative under stated criteria or a stated limit of the work.
          </p>
        </Apparatus>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2><span class="mark">Reproducibility</span>The site cannot change the data</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <ul>
            <li>
              <b>Datasets are frozen in numbered versions.</b> Buddhist Bridges is frozen at v3.0 with a
              SHA-256 manifest over 13 CSVs; 長恨 Issue 1 is frozen at corpus v3.3, with Issue 2 held as a
              working layer. Every figure can be checked against the exact state of the evidence it came from.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Export verifies the manifest before every build.</b> The Buddhist Bridges web export aborts
              on a checksum mismatch, so a change to the frozen release cannot reach the site silently.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>One writer per file.</b> The 長恨 site data is written only by the corpus build script, after
              a provenance validator has checked witness attestation, references and links. Two writers once
              raced on that file and silently reverted it; the rule exists because of that.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Display never edits the record.</b> Verified readings of damaged bylines are a display layer;
              the frozen corpus keeps the original in every case. Researcher instructions are stripped from
              public view, and scholarly uncertainty — queried readings, damaged text, unsigned items — is kept.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Attribution is not authorship.</b> A byline is evidence that a text was published under a
              name. Identities carry a certainty status, and pen names are never silently merged into people.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Static output, no servers.</b> Every page is pre-rendered HTML with no runtime database and
              no third-party requests, following the Endings Project principles for digital longevity.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2><span class="mark">Naming</span>Romanisation policy</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            The site uses <b>McCune-Reischauer</b>, because most of the material is premodern and colonial and
            the anglophone scholarship on it uses that system: a reader matching <i>Wŏnhyo</i> or
            <i>Ch'oe Nam-sŏn</i> against the literature should find them. Four exceptions apply, in order.
          </p>
          <ol class="notation">
            <li>A living person's own published spelling wins: Ko Un, Jung Seung-suk, Lokesh Chandra.</li>
            <li>Modern institutions as they style themselves: The Academy of Korean Studies, Yonsei University.</li>
            <li>Words naturalised in English keep their common form: Seoul, Korea, Hallyu, hangŭl, kisaeng, sijo, hanja.</li>
            <li>Everything else follows McCune-Reischauer, including dynasties: Chosŏn, Koryŏ, Silla, Paekche, Kyŏngsŏng.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            On first mention a Korean name is given as <b>Romanisation (한글, 漢字, dates)</b>. Titles of Korean
            works are given in their original script, with the romanisation once: 長恨 (Changhan).
            The term used throughout for the women of the registered entertainment profession is
            <i>kisaeng</i>, which is both the McCune-Reischauer form and the standard in the field's literature.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2><span class="mark">Figures</span>What a figure on this site must carry</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            No figure is published without all five of: a kicker naming it, a title stating the finding as a
            sentence, the chart itself, a caption explaining the encoding and its reach, and the underlying
            data as a table. Charts are hand-built SVG on this shared grammar, with no charting framework,
            because frameworks are what most often break a project a few years after it is finished.
          </p>
          <p class="small muted">
            The gap this addresses is documented: a 2024 survey of 186 web-based Digital Humanities projects
            found that 122 use maps, 12 critically adapt them, and none visualises uncertainty. See Tommaso
            Battisti, <i>Classification of web-based Digital Humanities projects leveraging information
            visualisation techniques</i> (Zenodo, 2024),
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.14192758">doi:10.5281/zenodo.14192758</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
<style>
  .grammar { max-width: var(--w-measure); margin: 24px 0 28px; border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
  .g-row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 130px 1fr; gap: 24px; align-items: center; padding: 16px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
  .g-row svg { width: 120px; height: 22px; display: block; }
  .g-row b { display: block; font: 600 14px/1.4 var(--f-ui); color: var(--heading); margin-bottom: 3px; }
  .g-row span { font: 400 14px/1.6 var(--f-ui); color: var(--text); }
  .notation { max-width: 66ch; }
  .notation code { font: 400 13px/1.5 var(--f-mono); background: var(--white); padding: 1px 6px; }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .g-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 10px; } }
</style>
